# Relieving the Pain...
Tamil title: வேதனையைப் பபோக்கியும்...

“What can he do except toil? He has no education; naturally he must live by hardship.”

A compassionate person may speak so of those whose bodies are worn down for a livelihood. Fleshly beings who refuse to recognise human worth speak without pity: “Fool! Your skull is full of clay. Do you imagine someone as wretched as you will find delicate work?” Those who enlist religion to stupefy the mind say, “What can be done? Such is your fate. What use blaming anyone?”

The tone differs, but all three admit two truths: many people labour in conditions unbearable to behold, doing hard and dangerous work; and they do not know how to end that suffering.

The autocrat provides no liberation. The exploiter deceives; the religious advocate points to another world. They do not answer whether people must suffer so greatly to live, whether human intelligence can devise liberation, or whether work must always be painful. It is not merely unwillingness; they do not know. Science came forward for this heroic task, promised to reduce suffering, and demonstrated in countless astonishing ways that it could.

A lover climbing two hundred steps to see Nalini on the fourth floor may, despite love, momentarily think of his aching legs. The electrically powered lift wonderfully removes that burden: he stands in a small enclosure, presses a button and reaches the top. This is one example of science relieving toil.

Beyond such a pleasant example, science has provided innumerable wonders to ease human work. Because they arrived without our own difficult search, we forget their value. Some small-minded people even ask what is remarkable about them. If people reflected on how greatly science reduced society's distress, they would not merely marvel but bow before it.

Human beings worked until blood dried, sinews broke, bodies wasted, eyes dimmed and limbs tired. Scientific methods reduced such labour and pain in countless tasks, freeing humanity from the prison of animal existence. Yet people forgot to respect the very science that liberated them.

Consider an ordinary five-watt electric lamp. How many oil lamps, how much time, labour and cost would be required to obtain the same light? Once people endured heat, smoke and pain while burning many torches. Today a touch yields light without that expenditure. Then consider flight, travel beneath the sea, and seeing or hearing distant events. Before science, how much labour would have been needed to obtain even one ten-thousandth of their present benefit? Science saved that labour and reduced the hardship.

Those who dismiss this achievement make the informed feel anger as well as surprise. Yet if electricity fails briefly in their town, the same people who praise olden times suffer, complain and grow restless. Science saved people worn away by labour, reducing work, time and cost while providing comforts previously unimaginable and advancing public welfare.

With science, one person can easily and quickly accomplish what once required a thousand working together at full strength. This means human toil has generally been reduced.

In cities such as Madras, we still see the misery of a worker hauling a handcart loaded with heavy goods. Yet a single person seated in shade drives a lorry carrying loads greater in weight and volume. The handcart makes a human into an animal; the lorry lets him remain human. The lorry is only a small emblem of science. Without it many more people would drag carts in sweat, darkness and bodily ruin.

This relief came not from sacrifice or yoga, occult power, divine grace or magical ink. While priests rolled rosaries in temples and said pain could only be endured, people unable to bear the sight of suffering thought, investigated, failed a thousand times without surrender, tried again and devised scientific methods. They continue reducing pain.

Even an occasional temple visitor knows the resident deity's name, and those who never visit repeat and praise its local legend. Yet when travelling by train we do not know, or think to learn, the inventor's name. We listen to radio and send an urgent telegram without asking who gave us those instruments. We enjoy the full assistance of science without awareness or gratitude. Worse, many now call scientific devices harmful, dangerous and unnecessary, citing Gandhi as witness.

If we understood science and felt compassion against wasting human labour, would we still seek the spinning wheel for cloth and mortar and pestle for rice, and call that national regeneration?

Two or three centuries ago, scientific weaving methods removed hardship, reduced labour and multiplied output. Today a fraction of the old toil yields many times the old result. Yet rulers are allowed to devise a spinning wheel for every household and insist people spin and weave their own clothing. Our provincial Premier goes further: each household should also grow the cotton needed for its yarn. How far from science does Prakasam Garu intend to retreat?

If told this cannot supply enough cloth, perhaps in greater anger he will say that only people who go outside need much clothing and order everyone to stay home. Our earliest ancestors lived in caves except when hunting or dancing the *kuravai*; perhaps he will preach that life too.

Even our present scientific facilities are meagre beside those abroad. Look at labourers on the Pallavaram hills near Madras, clinging to slopes, hanging from rope ladders and breaking stone under burning sun. Look at women breaking roadside stones. What labour and pain!

The American industrialist Ford said people still worked as slaves in Asia. Asked whom he meant, he cited those in places like India who survived by breaking stone and cutting wood. Their lives resembled slavery. In ancient times everywhere, humans shattered stone, cut timber and swept roads, wasting their bodies simply to live. Western societies expanded science through intellect and changed the condition in which every task demanded the sacrifice of human labour. They reduced work while increasing life's comforts.

There, more work is now performed with electricity, steam, coal and other forms of power than by human muscle. This is called the modern age, progress, human development and civilisation. Even here, the person free from brutal toil lives more civilly, while the “slave” labourer rolls in dust. Art scents the landlord's life; what fragrance enters Chinnan's life on the farm? Likewise, science has reduced the West's hard labour and filled life with art, while here we hear only anxiety.

India still depends more upon human muscle than other power, yet obtains very little output. The farmer lifting water manually labours more and produces less than one using a two- or three-horsepower pump. Machinery and science are companions that reduce human toil and increase results.

A 1929 calculation comparing human labour, coal, petroleum and water power exposed the people's pain. Around the world, rivers, forests and underground minerals became instruments of science, ending the cruelty of humans labouring like animals. Had capitalism not appropriated science for selfish ends, the world could already be a realm of happiness.

Without sweating and gasping through severe labour, human beings could work with intelligence and scientific tools, obtain many times today's result, toil less and live nobly. Science has opened that route. Foreign countries travelled it; contact with them placed this country beside the path, ready to enter. Gandhi calls, “Come back! We do not want the modern road; let us recover antiquity.” My question is not simply whether that appears right or wrong to him. Is it suitable for national progress? Is it the way to full life for the people?

Science gives humanity new strength and comfort in the struggle for life. Spinning-wheel propaganda is one fragment of the doctrine rejecting it. For now only textile mills are condemned, while the Premier simultaneously promises large industrial schemes. Why use machinery in other fields but send our country back to the age of the hand wheel only for weaving? He gives no coherent answer.

Unless checked at the beginning, today's mill-abolition plan will gradually spread into an effort to remove science from every sphere. The sign has appeared while people are distracted by a larger issue. The Premier planning to abolish textile mills also plans to abolish rice mills: no new rice mills will be permitted. A wheel for clothing and a pestle for rice, the second step has arrived. Next we must seek oil lamps, palm leaves and styluses, and inhabit wilderness.

This course results from refusing to recognise not only science's brilliance but also its social benefits: labour saved and hardship reduced. Other countries increasingly use water, fire, wind, electricity and natural forces rather than consuming human strength, while new attempts continue.

Machines powered by steam or electricity perform tasks from pumping water to pulling trains. We hear of three- and five-horsepower machines and see them in gardens and factories. Their meaning is a gift from intelligent humanity to reduce bodily ruin. Yet the Premier says we should not use it. Is that justice?

The article calculates that one horsepower equals the strength of twenty people. A small one-horsepower machine thus prevents twenty bodies from being worn down and greatly reduces the time required. A farmer who rises at cockcrow, carries pole and pot to the field and lifts water all day can irrigate only a fraction of the area a machine can. Why lose such instruments? Must humans labour like animals? Why refuse to apply scientific knowledge to natural forces when the rest of the world uses wind, fire, water and underground resources to ease life?

No one need merely say machinery is rejected: it is being demonstrated. The wheel for clothing and pestle for rice mean spinning work for the man and pounding work for the woman, while Prakasam takes the work of ruling. Is this intelligence? Angry supporters shout when questioned. Is that proper?

Scientists discovered “fire in water” and made flowing water an instrument for reducing human torment, not through incantation or Mahakali's grace, but intellect. Water power drives immense machines and accomplishes work that would require hundreds of thousands, saving labour and time while increasing results.

Mettur is a mountain and Kolar lies underground. Water from the heights produces electric lamps that turn the depths into broad daylight. We delight in stories of Arjuna descending to the netherworld and meeting a serpent maiden. Yet before our eyes, without Krishna's flute or Arjuna's arrow, rainwater is impounded by reason, released in the required amount and place, made to drive great machines and generate electricity that carries light across long distances. More than a marvel, this is a victory for intelligence and a means of reducing painful labour.

In Bombay, the Tata company dams water from the Western Ghats and generates 230,000 horsepower of electricity, described as work equivalent to 4.6 million people without those people being present. This power once rolled unused down the mountains as water. Poets sang the stream's murmur and flood, painters depicted it, and Vedantists used it to frighten people with analogies about drowning in pleasure. Who recognised its strength, imagined controlling it, and devised a plan to derive immense benefit? Science did.

These invisible millions light Bombay, operate fifty-three textile mills and run the train from Bombay to Poona. They are not Brahma's creations but magical workers intelligence drew from the river. Once wandering uncontrolled, they now obey commands. Which is better: using water power or returning to the hand wheel and hand pestle? An angry Congress supporter asks who wishes to return to the forest age. Do not be angry, friend. The journey begins by saying no mill for clothing and no machine for rice; if allowed, it ends in that forest age.

The source gives a national total for electrical horsepower and equates it with the work of roughly thirty million people. It then says many more hidden “magical workers” exist as water, fire and coal. Scientists estimated that water alone could provide power equal to 540 million people, while India's population was forty crores. Science's task was to summon these helpers; the spinning wheel and pestle campaign began a war to prevent their arrival.

Energy can also be obtained from coal by making fire, power and steam. Steam rises from beneath the earth too: Italy was said to generate 4,000 horsepower of electricity from geothermal steam. Even the sun's heat can be made to work. A small electric machine had been experimentally operated from solar heat, and research continued. Yet in precisely these days, rulers spoke against machines: the opening chapter was the declaration that mills were unnecessary, and the country listened.

Published in *Dravida Nadu*, 20 October; the OCR-rendered year is corrupt.

---NOTES---
The Tamil OCR source is retained verbatim. Its title appears with a duplicated character in “பபோக்கியும்,” which has not been silently altered; the English follows the evident sense. Several numerical strings are internally inconsistent because OCR has inserted or omitted digits, especially the all-India horsepower figure and corresponding human-labour equivalent. The English states only quantities recoverable from the surrounding Tamil wording and explicitly labels the uncertain national total. “Horsepower” equivalences and technological claims are translated as the source's period argument, not independently validated scientific conversions. The publication year in “20-10-4567” is unreadable as a reliable date. Names and terms such as Prakasam Garu, Mettur, Kolar, Tata, Poona, *kuravai* and *kadam* are rendered conservatively.
